  "lens": {
   "mechanics": "Gold competes with cash and bonds as a store of wealth. Holding it gives up the real yield, the return after inflation, so a rising real yield makes gold costlier to hold and inflation above yields makes it cheaper.",
   "data": "The template says gold loses when real yields rise and money stays tight. Three forces today. First, the real yield rose 3bp to 2.67%. Second, a 5.05% 10-year still beats 3.7% CPI, so bonds pay a real return. Third, 4.2% unemployment and 194k claims let the Fed stay tight, and two officials voted to hike on 28 Oct (Fed). All three lean against gold. The difference: spot held near $4,050, while the breakeven rose 3bp to 2.38%, so a geopolitical bid the template leaves out seems to be at work. Rough gauges.",
   "unsustainable": "Federal debt is 6.52 times revenue and interest takes 21.1% of it, like a family whose interest bill eats a fifth of its pay and grows with every rate rise.",
   "would_change": "September core PCE below the 0.3% m/m forecast, or Q3 GDP well under the 3.2% forecast (Forex Factory), both at 20:30 HKT today.",
